\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38}{\ignorespaces A curved element of degree two. Left: the reference triangle with its six nodes, three vertices and three edge nodes. Middle: its image on the unit sphere under the quadratic map $F$ through six points of the sphere, with the moving frame of the element, $e_1 = \partial _1 F/|\partial _1 F|$ (red) and $e_2 = n \times e_1$ (blue), at a few points: the frame turns inside the element, and its connection form $\omega $ measures how. Right: where the curvature of a mesh of such elements lives: inside the elements ($K\, dA$, shaded), on the edges (the jump $[\kappa _g]$ of the geodesic curvature of the shared edge curve, red), and at the vertices (the angle defect $\kappa _v$ of the curved corners, dot), (\ref {eqn:p2_gauss_bonnet}).}}{90}{figure.caption.95}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:p2_element}{{38}{90}{A curved element of degree two. Left: the reference triangle with its six nodes, three vertices and three edge nodes. Middle: its image on the unit sphere under the quadratic map $F$ through six points of the sphere, with the moving frame of the element, $e_1 = \partial _1 F/|\partial _1 F|$ (red) and $e_2 = n \times e_1$ (blue), at a few points: the frame turns inside the element, and its connection form $\omega $ measures how. Right: where the curvature of a mesh of such elements lives: inside the elements ($K\, dA$, shaded), on the edges (the jump $[\kappa _g]$ of the geodesic curvature of the shared edge curve, red), and at the vertices (the angle defect $\kappa _v$ of the curved corners, dot), (\ref {eqn:p2_gauss_bonnet})}{figure.caption.95}{}}
